        "id": 1021820
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-31T23:17:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, that\u0027s right. C\u0027s `enum` types are effectively just integers with named constants for each member, whereas Rust `enum`s have a limited set of valid values, so there\u0027s either a UB problem or a validation problem when using the latter to model the former. The naming difference is also awkward. So indeed we just use a type alias and a bunch of constants, and then `match` on them where appropriate. One nice advantage of this approach is that we don\u0027t need to implement `GuestRet`/`GuestArg` etc, they\u0027re re-used because of the type alias.",
      "parentUuid": "4f0dc931_977f91c4",
      "revId": "56d3c1f6371872d30936f4b49184e1641caa336f",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
